PicoGKRuntime 26.2 (0f26321c) — read access to LatticeBeam / LatticeSphere
parameters, upstreamable to leap71/PicoGKRuntime verbatim.

Applied to vendor/PicoGKRuntime by scripts/fetch-deps.sh. Kept as a patch
because it modifies dependency source; no flag-level override exists.

Source/PicoGKLattice.h — seven inline const accessors. LatticeBeam and
LatticeSphere expose only fSdValue() and a bounding box, so the ONLY way to
render a lattice is to ask each element for a signed distance at a point. That
forces every renderer into the shape Voxels::RenderLattice already has: a
serial dense-accessor loop over per-beam bounding boxes
(PicoGKVdbVoxels.h:343-358). An alternative renderer needs the defining
parameters instead — openvdb's own tube-complex voxelizer
(tools::createLevelSetTubeComplex, LevelSetTubes.h) takes vertex, segment and
radius ARRAYS and voxelizes them with TBB, and a GPU lane wants the same flat
arrays for upload.

Value-neutral by construction: seven `inline ... const` getters returning
members, no member added, no member changed, no existing declaration touched
(a pure insertion — `diff` shows only `+` lines). The class layouts, the
`protected:` sections and nMemUsage()'s sizeof arithmetic are all unchanged.

Consumer: src/pico-lattice.cpp (SK-0.4). Evidence:
bench/results/webgpu-v2/sk-0.4-*.json.

--- a/Source/PicoGKLattice.h
+++ b/Source/PicoGKLattice.h
@@ -70,6 +70,13 @@
     
     inline const BBox3& oBBox() const       {return m_oBBox;}
     
+    // Read access to the defining parameters. fSdValue() is the only way to ask
+    // this sphere anything, which forces every renderer into a per-sample query.
+    // An alternative renderer needs the parameters themselves.
+    inline const Vector3& vecCenter() const {return m_vecC;}
+    
+    inline float fRadius() const            {return m_fRad;}
+    
 protected:
     
     Vector3 m_vecC;
@@ -113,6 +120,20 @@
     
     inline const BBox3& oBBox() const       {return m_oBBox;}
     
+    // Read access to the defining parameters -- see the note on LatticeSphere.
+    // A round-capped beam is exactly the convex hull of the two end spheres, so
+    // (vecStart, vecEnd, fRadStart, fRadEnd) is all a vectorised or GPU renderer
+    // needs; bRoundCap() tells it whether the flat-cone form applies instead.
+    inline const Vector3& vecStart() const  {return m_vecS;}
+    
+    inline const Vector3& vecEnd() const    {return m_vecE;}
+    
+    inline float fRadStart() const          {return m_fRadS;}
+    
+    inline float fRadEnd() const            {return m_fRadE;}
+    
+    inline bool bRoundCap() const           {return m_bRoundCap;}
+    
 protected:
     
     float fSdvRoundCone(const Vector3& p) const
